WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.783
- Good morning and welcome.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.100
My name is Sharon Kugler,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.780
and I'm the Yale University chaplain.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.500
It's a true privilege to assist

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.600
with this service of remembrance

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.670
honoring those among the first women

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.270
in Yale College who have
died before they could mark

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.373
this 50 year milestone.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.160
As I visited the extraordinary exhibit

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.960
over at the Sterling
Memorial Library last week,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.730
it was striking to see
how much the first women

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.660
who came to Yale to get an education

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.773
actually educated Yale.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.140
What they endured, what you endured,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.160
and how you became the women

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.540
whose remarkable minds and hearts

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.700
have touched and improved the world

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.953
is most humbling and deeply inspiring.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.560
The generations of women
that have and will continue

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.170
to follow you do not walk in your shadow.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.573
They are walking in your light.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.670
This morning, we have
come together to carve out

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.543
a little time and space
to nurture our hearts.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.850
There's much to celebrate
outside of these walls,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.440
and yet, we are also
mindful that our circle

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.853
of celebration is incomplete.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:27.670
In the midst of the
festivities of this weekend,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.183
our hearts also ache.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.363
There are pieces of us,
sisters, who are missing.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.570
For a little while, we will
take some time to pause

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.530
in loving remembrance for
those who have left this earth

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.330
and are not here to
share in the many facets

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.313
of this weekend.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.860
We miss their voices here,
and we honor their memory

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.103
as being part of the firsts.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.880
Let this space be a comfort to you

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.863
as your thoughts drift
wherever they need to be.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.650
May you feel welcome to
participate in any way

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.313
that brings you a sense
of peace in this moment.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.260
We have brought together
different elements

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.090
for this service.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.913
There is much music to soothe,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.010
some spoken word and
poetry, prayer and prose,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.103
a lovingly curated backdrop,
and most importantly,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.540
there'll be quiet time and space

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.340
for you to hold yourself
and one another gently

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.143
as you remember those who have died.

00:02:35.395 --> 00:02:37.845
We have lit a candle to
bring light to each name.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.323
Some of our sisters left very young,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.443
others had more life, more decades.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.533
I invite you now to breathe in and out,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.930
to be fully present in this space

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.563
with yourself and one another.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.663
Let us begin.

